[Music]  my name is Bart Ehrman and I'm professor  philosophy at the University of  Groningen in the Netherlands you're  interested in error theory in the  normative judgments how did you come to  become interested in in this kind of  disagreement I guess when I entered  philosophy I was most interested in  political philosophy so I originally  started out as an economics student and  then I moved into political philosophy  and I guess when I did that it really  bothered me that it sort of wasn't clear  to me whether there were any facts of  the matter about what justices say or  what is what rights people have and so  on so even though initially I was  interested in more in political issues I  gradually moved into more foundational  philosophical philosophical issues and  that's how I became interested in in  meta ethics which is the area of  philosophy that asks foundational  questions about ethics and I guess sort  of the holy grail of meta ethics is the  question is morality objective in some  way or is it subjective in some way and  obviously to answer that question you  have to try to be really clear what you  mean when you call something objective  or subjective so a large part of  metaphysics is about those sorts of  issues so when when do we have  objectivity what exactly is required for  objectivity so that was what I was  initially interested in and I guess I  was initially more attracted to another  view in meta ethics which is known as  realism so realism says that there are  actually facts of the matter about  what's right and what's wrong or what  people have reasons to do and so on but  I guess after having been a committed  realist for quite a few years I became  to doubt more and more that we could  actually make sense of this and so  that's how I gradually moved into an  error theory in ethics and so the error  theory is a view that says that moral  judgments so thoughts about what's right  and wrong what people have reason to do  what we should do what our obligations  are and so on  so the error theory says that those  judgments are sort of attempts to  represent the world as being a certain  way so they ascribe properties to things  in the world so they say of things in  the world that they're right or wrong or  obligatory and so on but then the error  theory says well actually those  properties don't actually exist so there  is no such thing as wrongness or  permissibility or obligations and  therefore all moral judgments are false  and then what I did was well I I thought  well actually the arguments for that  sort of theory generalized beyond moral  judgments I now think and they also  apply to what philosophers call other  normative judgments so that means not  just thoughts about what's right and  wrong and what but what's morally  obligatory and so on but also thoughts  about what there's reason to do in  general what there's reason to believe  what's rational and so on so that's how  I came to defend a narrow theory about  all monetary judgments rather than just  moral judgments the other thing I  believe about the error theory is that  we can't actually believe this very  general error theory so that's another  thing I've I've argued for and the  reason for that is that this very  general error theory applies to all  normative judgments so it also applies  to judgments about reasons or belief so  there's very general error theory says  that there's actually no reason to  believe that error theory  so in order to believe that's very  general our theory is have to believe  that there's no reason to believe the  arrow theory and that is something that  I think we can't do so I think we can't  have a belief and at the same time fully  and consciously and explicitly believed  that there's absolutely no reason to  have that belief so I think we can't  believe the arrow theory and what that  means is that the truth of the arrow  theory isn't going to have any effect  whatsoever on how we make moral  decisions so we should make moral  decisions I think in the way we've  always done I don't believe the arrow  theory myself either so I think we  should just you know look at the pros  and cons of the different options  weighed them up and come to a decision  about what what the best thing to do is  or what the right thing to do is but at  the end of the day leave some place for  uncertainty about believing that this is  the right thing to do absolutely but I  think that is something that anybody  should do really so a lot of realists  would also say I think that you should  never be too sure about your moral  opinions and in fact a lot of realists  would say well we are the people who  could really explain why you shouldn't  be too sure about your moral opinions  because there are facts of the matter  out there and so you might get them  wrong so that is something I think that  anybody working in meta attics would  want to preserve that there is room for  uncertainty but then again if like me  you think that the best arguments in  meta ethics seem to show that this error  theory is true maybe that's an  additional reason for some modesty as to  whether you've got things right even  though you don't believe the error  theory you might you might get close to  believing here in theory  so that might be an additional reason to  be a bit modest about whether you've got  things right how does that affect  politics because you were in at first  interested in philosophy right yeah I'm  not sure actually that's something I'm  thinking about right now so so one thing  I'm interested in is whether so there's  this famous philosopher in philosophy  John Rawls who defended a distinctive  form of liberalism which asks citizens  to bracket certain opinions that they  might have about the good life and I  guess one live debate in philosophy is  whether that presupposes a certain kind  of skepticism about good life and maybe  about morality in general and whether  that kind of skepticism then goes on to  undermining the kind of political view  defenders so one thing I'm thinking  about right now is whether maybe this  unbelievable error theory in the tactics  might help us to say something useful in  defense of the kind of liberalism that  rolls to defend but I should say I'm not  at all sure whether that is going to  work because obviously the danger is  always going to be if you defend  anti-realism about something  normative it's very hard to move from  there to some form of liberalism or  tolerance because liberalism or  tolerance are also going to be it's  going to be the claim that we should be  liberals and we should be tolerance and  that's going to be another long tip  plank that will be undermined if you're  if you're anti-realism is is true so  that's a very difficult thing to do  to carry out and I'm not at all sure  that I'm able to do it but that's  something I'm thinking about at the  moment and what what is your research  focusing at the moment well at the  moment so I I've written a book in  defense of the arrow theory and arguing  that we can't believe the theory so one  thing I'm doing right now is responding  to challenges that people have made to  the arguments in the book and also  thinking a bit further about certain  premises and the arguments that I  thought didn't need to be defended but  that I now see do need some additional  defense so that's one thing that I'm  doing  could you give examples of them one  premise is so in the book I assume that  the normative supervenes on the  descriptive in a certain way and what  that means is that there can't be any  changes in the normative status that  actions have to accompany any changes in  their rightness or wrongness and so on  if those changes are not accompanied by  certain more normative changes so for  example if an action that is right if  that action is to become wrong and there  must be some other change in the world  that makes it the case that the action  that used to be right is now is how  wrong so I used to assume that that was  just completely uncontroversial but  there are now philosophers who are  challenging that assumption and very  well I'm not convinced by the challenge  but it's obviously something that I need  to think more about so that's one of the  things I'm not working on and have you  recently changed your mind about an idea  well I guess it would be this miss  supervenience idea I mean the thing is  so it's a technical philosophical notion  but if you spell it out in a non  philosophical intuitive way it just  seems totally obvious right so how can  an action that is right become wrong  without some other change in the world  it can't just be bear a bear changing  like this or enormous so I used to think  that was obvious and as a result of  reading what people have said about this  I've changed my mind and I now think  that that really needs needs defense and  that happens a lot in philosophy so if  you if you try to do philosophy well  then you'll have to pay attention to  what other people are saying and you  have to read their work and it will  happen quite often that maybe you don't  completely change your mind about a  broad topic but you will change your  mind about you know how I'll be  something is whether something needs  defense whether a certain argument works  or doesn't work so that happens quite a  bit and if you were to choose one  classic philosopher that has really  influenced you who would that be  so do you mean like can it be a  reasonably contemporary philosopher oh  yeah right okay so so the the the  philosopher who really made me think  that I wants to be a philosopher myself  was Derek Parfit so he was philosopher  at Oxford he died a few years ago and as  an undergraduate I read his book reasons  and persons and that made a huge  impression on me  not so much for the conclusions that he  reached but for the way he reached those  conclusion so reasons in person is a  really big book